 to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-1$ and $x=2.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/9e6e1147d95c65465c802e8bfad5c4ac285dc9716d098452c41bdd6caed5b9ca.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2-{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(2-{x}^{2})-(-x)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+x+2)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-80186/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee633017-5c17-575f-bdab-5f12b7319355"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80186"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-3121/selection/c700317676adba57637b8c69a9441ed34a314f0fa030d3f57d983c2a15f581b3" :answer/id #uuid "4f880dfe-cf92-5630-a407-fb84613d0779" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(2{x}^{2}+x-1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-3121/selection/8e6a171334f3ae76aa3cfe599120194a084d1b6960231029836b7517400b74a8" :answer/id #uuid "3af74cc8-e984-5307-932a-d1e2de0ecbe2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-x-1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-3121/selection/6d1be6c90a4d614faf9b9358fa07cd18600f3ded2ce76c9f1baa65465d5429d1" :answer/id #uuid "3c29da1f-9850-5b4d-a908-1c5101e99420" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x+1-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-3121/selection/f5f0ad94e914d486b3c5f8bd1be04758813e01963eb78f54c886365a3b5b15ba" :answer/id #uuid "5b1ec612-898d-50c9-bb3c-0d699ac52d41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x-1-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-3121/selection/463e5cb107c1bde132dee07a2bcbbdf14bae82512f8179101d30ffd939740969" :answer/id #uuid "f68fe51f-a4d9-5f8f-aa10-f2e8fa70fec9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x-1+2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-3121/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3d27a92d-a819-5dbc-8fab-b0aa6bc376dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-3121/selection/6d1be6c90a4d614faf9b9358fa07cd18600f3ded2ce76c9f1baa65465d5429d1" :answer-field/choices ["q-3121/selection/c700317676adba57637b8c69a9441ed34a314f0fa030d3f57d983c2a15f581b3" "q-3121/selection/8e6a171334f3ae76aa3cfe599120194a084d1b6960231029836b7517400b74a8" "q-3121/selection/6d1be6c90a4d614faf9b9358fa07cd18600f3ded2ce76c9f1baa65465d5429d1" "q-3121/selection/f5f0ad94e914d486b3c5f8bd1be04758813e01963eb78f54c886365a3b5b15ba" "q-3121/selection/463e5cb107c1bde132dee07a2bcbbdf14bae82512f8179101d30ffd939740969"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-3121" :question/id #uuid "c69be2ea-7b70-598d-bb9a-251384a82770" :question/math-academy-id "q-3121" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/9a87ae2b230e66b685051ee7d13987f5f68690a07a67ade9e7af0eb870f6224f.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=2{x}^{2}$ and $y=x+1,$ as shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=1.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/e77170aac6d81b1cfd0e93804b0c59802f28089d24aeaff065da04cabc44c172.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=2{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x+1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}[\\,(x+1)-(2{x}^{2})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x+1-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-3121/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee633017-5c17-575f-bdab-5f12b7319355"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-3121"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-49880/selection/d34b7174ce4834496a8828464e7cf464bc3a5647d5dbb4e0fc75392392deac00" :answer/id #uuid "1cfb3d58-3d5e-5c79-9366-227dbad3db20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{8}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x-8)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49880/selection/9e45d521b0395007d0812f70bf384e5185c6848d910c04f0bf6ea2b35c0b0ce9" :answer/id #uuid "4f63bde5-58e2-5d40-874e-044bd20c6e3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{4}({x}^{2}+x+8)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49880/selection/66c8ca99a688623963cb458d5cd5724177aa3ae96b6eaf2ef6b9db860189aa11" :answer/id #uuid "53848356-a06a-5fe2-ab1e-50d4c90e64d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{8}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49880/selection/43f31480e008137ba36e8af1ca9cc78ca023a634205172c18ce6a84f4b0374ca" :answer/id #uuid "c9b9e3b5-1d8a-5778-9ade-73b2ac6b7e86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{8}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+x+8)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49880/selection/f66dd57b8d4c195e2f5acd8ab249df9701d8dfd626821cdb7cb0cd5bce60a80e" :answer/id #uuid "a7ef0012-cb76-560a-9970-0e43b64287fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{4}(x+6)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49880/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "24b9eb88-d29f-5222-a148-5ec7f854d5aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-49880/selection/43f31480e008137ba36e8af1ca9cc78ca023a634205172c18ce6a84f4b0374ca" :answer-field/choices ["q-49880/selection/d34b7174ce4834496a8828464e7cf464bc3a5647d5dbb4e0fc75392392deac00" "q-49880/selection/9e45d521b0395007d0812f70bf384e5185c6848d910c04f0bf6ea2b35c0b0ce9" "q-49880/selection/66c8ca99a688623963cb458d5cd5724177aa3ae96b6eaf2ef6b9db860189aa11" "q-49880/selection/43f31480e008137ba36e8af1ca9cc78ca023a634205172c18ce6a84f4b0374ca" "q-49880/selection/f66dd57b8d4c195e2f5acd8ab249df9701d8dfd626821cdb7cb0cd5bce60a80e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-49880" :question/id #uuid "72208a61-59e2-5d43-bb42-bed25d98e203" :question/math-academy-id "q-49880" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/1e75b221cacd3d2bad25065fe13bbf77f07fa711fe7f865358ff181039ced560.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=x+6$ and $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2$, shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-4$ and $x=8.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/56ec4a3e3bd4b8b652832d4b32ba730717a49ab2438ef2c978ea99b291a7f462.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $f(x)=x+6.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{8}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{8}[\\,(x+6)-(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{8}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+x+8)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-49880/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee633017-5c17-575f-bdab-5f12b7319355"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49880"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-121526/selection/fc255c38962e5bef254d52554038812dfcfb651f9caf80cf2ab5f8aa52af0d68" :answer/id #uuid "71d53f25-d523-54c4-a780-b7ab3274cfef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{1}({x}^{2}-4)\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121526/selection/e58fe20501be1dfc5f2398f8088067d0eb0ba11d466ed3211cd64130990f195f" :answer/id #uuid "3f8bb39b-43c1-5fbd-a398-407183920383" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(8-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121526/selection/a01b65bb3908b4785ff4142ae042ee99717e6c8eebc2cb7678f8bcea996b9469" :answer/id #uuid "a3647819-fcc7-5680-861d-bd11d552a8a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121526/selection/891024c0cbd36483b2a958eea8586e5b1b7cef6a2f0877a4ff03d010afd76e83" :answer/id #uuid "fae4c85b-01f7-585f-9179-04329a1fbb59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16+4{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121526/selection/62e18b052f5b4820d527442e514d60c907ebf6bcdf3482ba81948db73db52fc8" :answer/id #uuid "2392df36-67f9-5e27-a0c6-6b0026c175f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{4}(4-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121526/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6b339274-4f6a-5f2b-92df-78c9f65fe43b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-121526/selection/a01b65bb3908b4785ff4142ae042ee99717e6c8eebc2cb7678f8bcea996b9469" :answer-field/choices ["q-121526/selection/fc255c38962e5bef254d52554038812dfcfb651f9caf80cf2ab5f8aa52af0d68" "q-121526/selection/e58fe20501be1dfc5f2398f8088067d0eb0ba11d466ed3211cd64130990f195f" "q-121526/selection/a01b65bb3908b4785ff4142ae042ee99717e6c8eebc2cb7678f8bcea996b9469" "q-121526/selection/891024c0cbd36483b2a958eea8586e5b1b7cef6a2f0877a4ff03d010afd76e83" "q-121526/selection/62e18b052f5b4820d527442e514d60c907ebf6bcdf3482ba81948db73db52fc8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-121526" :question/id #uuid "f1267085-eeb4-5b23-9c27-dc0dee8c5147" :question/math-academy-id "q-121526" :question/problem "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded between $y={x}^{2}+2x-9$ and $y=2x+7?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the graphs of the two functions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/2514c3e7bf5352dbe26dddc479f66042b3736b93f82b97a60850f768a89a4e17.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-9 & =2x+7 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-4$ to $x=4.$\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/560fb0937828d5afb5de9593280a1177773598c5d3534ee26318e0bc2788f5ab.png)\n\n \n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}+2x-9,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2x+7.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(2x+7)-({x}^{2}+2x-9)\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(-{x}^{2}+16)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-121526/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e016cedd-0206-555c-8934-c6d19566d547"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-121526"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-11487/selection/989630015fc5adbc01da0a7b9b116aad9f3682bde2b3c0f092e7ed5e4b7d50ca" :answer/id #uuid "fc78b8a5-decd-5153-98a2-48f1e78f2d67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{4}({x}^{2}-4x-5)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-11487/selection/b4c66c83ee79d72d1428a0826bc46da773f8fb80f124596a893e64f5b7a4c704" :answer/id #uuid "5ffc81ca-faa8-5d1c-bb8a-2c0898e4c2fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{5}({x}^{2}-4x-5)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-11487/selection/62408c8731fc99c6a763cdb6a9464ec0760d6d2bbb8d37832ac3f576d0cf5728" :answer/id #uuid "15b73c36-3bdc-5603-8ee1-16a746763542" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{5}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-11487/selection/5b238b02cbb6a5c47585f415d21332e961e8f2016d4e7e65e96428731cb704a4" :answer/id #uuid "cfac9120-eb2d-5507-b553-90da7e76cd63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{4}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-11487/selection/d2a55455e698c6644f52fabc1e6669773607da6d95b53e5311c6163e239508f7" :answer/id #uuid "24197187-5ffd-5de5-adcb-49c45ff22388" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{5}({x}^{2}-4x-5)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-11487/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "16e9d5af-1c25-5005-9231-15a7611d204a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-11487/selection/62408c8731fc99c6a763cdb6a9464ec0760d6d2bbb8d37832ac3f576d0cf5728" :answer-field/choices ["q-11487/selection/989630015fc5adbc01da0a7b9b116aad9f3682bde2b3c0f092e7ed5e4b7d50ca" "q-11487/selection/b4c66c83ee79d72d1428a0826bc46da773f8fb80f124596a893e64f5b7a4c704" "q-11487/selection/62408c8731fc99c6a763cdb6a9464ec0760d6d2bbb8d37832ac3f576d0cf5728" "q-11487/selection/5b238b02cbb6a5c47585f415d21332e961e8f2016d4e7e65e96428731cb704a4" "q-11487/selection/d2a55455e698c6644f52fabc1e6669773607da6d95b53e5311c6163e239508f7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-11487" :question/id #uuid "dd8d0f5b-37a6-5193-9c93-2abd275a5141" :question/math-academy-id "q-11487" :question/problem "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded between $y={x}^{2}-5$ and $y=4x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/b25bee7ef7257a5611d708eb65b3c8b453b230ad6d2e9289f6cb40dffba89277.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ={x}^{2}-5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x-5 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=5.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/bf79cec9c1ede4a95b3e20415fbf37224ca8616426e8530043fa4a4b54780bab.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=4x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}[\\,(4x)-({x}^{2}-5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}(-{x}^{2}+4x+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-11487/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e016cedd-0206-555c-8934-c6d19566d547"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-11487"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-49800/selection/5b1131c3b2e64d7537b69603d50ac56194af4b08dbde2458bad75429a6ea58f0" :answer/id #uuid "f9e13dbf-c306-58bc-a7e5-353ad7533ee4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}(-2{x}^{2}+6x-4)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49800/selection/52b60d5bf9d5e6d79a1d0243c105757e106ba2b0324161d073f8db8f3406c592" :answer/id #uuid "92f35698-fbf6-554a-a6d4-e3192b4628c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{4/3}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49800/selection/06133df223e489f4675d0a275b48d8f4165c8c0b2176b1cca6365bd01f0b6453" :answer/id #uuid "f60f4407-4c88-511d-891a-c3cffb94fb8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4/3}(3x-2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49800/selection/3ce5675d9e57882b91c6e794b15e16d445af06f9de8c80842be68dfb6a8070be" :answer/id #uuid "7f6fdf07-4470-58e0-83c6-bec3fccf1cde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-6x+4)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49800/selection/f371a28367453fc413ede4dae99fc69c6eedc4f6980ee2eefaec7ecb12e5af7b" :answer/id #uuid "64680e23-c2ad-5159-9b07-d9ab2b534e74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4/3}(2{x}^{2}-x-2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49800/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "508d5843-753f-5e35-b011-e4f47b56bb91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-49800/selection/5b1131c3b2e64d7537b69603d50ac56194af4b08dbde2458bad75429a6ea58f0" :answer-field/choices ["q-49800/selection/5b1131c3b2e64d7537b69603d50ac56194af4b08dbde2458bad75429a6ea58f0" "q-49800/selection/52b60d5bf9d5e6d79a1d0243c105757e106ba2b0324161d073f8db8f3406c592" "q-49800/selection/06133df223e489f4675d0a275b48d8f4165c8c0b2176b1cca6365bd01f0b6453" "q-49800/selection/3ce5675d9e57882b91c6e794b15e16d445af06f9de8c80842be68dfb6a8070be" "q-49800/selection/f371a28367453fc413ede4dae99fc69c6eedc4f6980ee2eefaec7ecb12e5af7b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-49800" :question/id #uuid "b895499b-b902-5259-aaab-68a4f2e466b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-49800" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/b5e422ab61b4ebcacafcadc082a1b85ebb453a689654a9cef7bc7e7a3a4721a3.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=f(x)=2{x}^{2}-3x+2$ and $y=g(x)=3x-2$, shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-3x+2 & =3x-2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6x+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/7d96f51f01c2e52756fe53a6b3a11dd5c7c6dd9cb15ce674a706b442a3e6ad2b.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-3x+2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $g(x)=3x-2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(3x-2)-(2{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(-2{x}^{2}+6x-4)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-49800/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e016cedd-0206-555c-8934-c6d19566d547"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49800"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-49885"] :question/problem "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=4{x}^{2}-x$ and $y=2-{x}^{2}-4x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/913ec93445f3ed571fcf98ad762eba2a1bc657dce7442c8e48fc4d5f36817def.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-x & =2-{x}^{2}-4x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+3x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot (-2)}}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm 7}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/77493917a13269c78acef8ff6fe57b5f350020542805e316f82f7a17dabedd24.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=4{x}^{2}-x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2-{x}^{2}-4x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}[\\,(2-{x}^{2}-4x)-(4{x}^{2}-x)\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}(-5{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-121512/selection/839b9965df2263103591520267206d09af18c848ce5d5cc576e1e03a9dcc3b2e" :answer/id #uuid "08d2a3d0-fefc-5d13-86e4-568953213391" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}(-5{x}^{2}+x+6)\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121512/selection/d252828ddfff98a35015bf1600adb0321acdbaf753a69f6d4fab9de7392a3253" :answer/id #uuid "d6683605-25f4-534a-9e56-69e2d3a9e473" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1/5}^{6/5}(-5{x}^{2}-x+2)\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121512/selection/af610d058e837beb2287d61f8c3e3b7d018829df41d624d6587f6d6ec7dcde8d" :answer/id #uuid "ebb25768-4002-514b-bbdf-1848601fdcc2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1/5}({x}^{2}+x+3)\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121512/selection/c9c8de5329a92a91242330d76ad3f14bce1378de2051bf60e9620c70dd6186f4" :answer/id #uuid "372b9448-e262-5710-a406-4a4fd99da531" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}(5{x}^{2}+x+4)\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121512/selection/0d57b2e1459b740306710793e6b79dfc3fd71d91529651d97973c781f9405059" :answer/id #uuid "18287057-ea0a-518c-9691-c7d0408a9617" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{6/5}(-5{x}^{2}+5x-6)\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121512/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e70b1c5b-29e7-59b7-b280-86a6990dddb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-121512/selection/839b9965df2263103591520267206d09af18c848ce5d5cc576e1e03a9dcc3b2e" :answer-field/choices ["q-121512/selection/839b9965df2263103591520267206d09af18c848ce5d5cc576e1e03a9dcc3b2e" "q-121512/selection/d252828ddfff98a35015bf1600adb0321acdbaf753a69f6d4fab9de7392a3253" "q-121512/selection/af610d058e837beb2287d61f8c3e3b7d018829df41d624d6587f6d6ec7dcde8d" "q-121512/selection/c9c8de5329a92a91242330d76ad3f14bce1378de2051bf60e9620c70dd6186f4" "q-121512/selection/0d57b2e1459b740306710793e6b79dfc3fd71d91529651d97973c781f9405059"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-121512" :question/id #uuid "d3a05e1c-a69f-56e3-b571-e32c1197e412" :question/math-academy-id "q-121512" :question/problem "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=3{x}^{2}-5x$ and $y=6-2{x}^{2}-4x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/813677aa1b12481e990058b129cb9ad24945a3dfb818afacfb2ecb720d6b81d3.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-5x & =6-2{x}^{2}-4x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-x-6 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot (-6)}}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm 11}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=\\frac{6}{5}.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/a8c538d5f40f68506588695aa04b38f095360b6f51369d8169099806a9678de9.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=3{x}^{2}-5x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=6-2{x}^{2}-4x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}[\\,(6-2{x}^{2}-4x)-(3{x}^{2}-5x)\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}(-5{x}^{2}+x+6)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-121512/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e016cedd-0206-555c-8934-c6d19566d547"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-121512"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-346621/field-1/d8180fb40af5867fc6ed0537e9ad01a16006bf53f8c78b6e3cc5f547b07ffefb" :answer/id #uuid "b4d18c80-2a7e-5607-9570-a2b1ce6478ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-346621/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0029ae7e-f00e-594c-aaab-b3d91b97df04" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-346621/field-1/d8180fb40af5867fc6ed0537e9ad01a16006bf53f8c78b6e3cc5f547b07ffefb" :answer-field/choices ["q-346621/field-1/d8180fb40af5867fc6ed0537e9ad01a16006bf53f8c78b6e3cc5f547b07ffefb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-346621" :question/id #uuid "bbf20fe3-aec0-56b8-9300-f9d1b858cbfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-346621" :question/problem "Find the area $A$ of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}-5$ and $y=11.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/d2398c3a8a04d005dd4d0c6e8b06046a6ca98aa86127204e7521341213dfb464.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5 & =11 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-4$ to $x=4.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/caf1c4d5df49aa18ed85158615be19745abb7c078269dde35f4e57d57fae292a.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=11.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(11)-({x}^{2}-5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(16x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =[16\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}]-[16\\cdot (-4)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-4)^{3}] \\\\  & =(64-\\frac{64}{3})-(-64+\\frac{64}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-346621/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa1bebf-4af0-5a5c-962c-8a88f8392776"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-346621"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-3146/selection/348159c05aa3af806578d05799a926b10947a5c2c1148eb2f22911b06a9a0c63" :answer/id #uuid "9009f62a-8f9f-541f-aaa9-3096b237215e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-3146/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "a9694e31-220c-5016-a56c-a6d1ed8cbfa5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-3146/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "49511dfd-2f1a-5934-9ab9-5d081ce4476d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-3146/selection/1114f773249b584f03ae90d8348b5b14abd566a3f244c0aad51e974a9460892e" :answer/id #uuid "66e4c855-4581-5a3a-91b4-ea2c82c9b481" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-3146/selection/07791849728418b50d59d705e3d959e93383e408886fd319ae3a513a1b7159ba" :answer/id #uuid "a09d3698-cee1-5a99-8f63-6d930b6b8555" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-3146/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "534945ed-8eee-5cf5-bf23-7e34d5e5fac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-3146/selection/07791849728418b50d59d705e3d959e93383e408886fd319ae3a513a1b7159ba" :answer-field/choices ["q-3146/selection/348159c05aa3af806578d05799a926b10947a5c2c1148eb2f22911b06a9a0c63" "q-3146/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-3146/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" "q-3146/selection/1114f773249b584f03ae90d8348b5b14abd566a3f244c0aad51e974a9460892e" "q-3146/selection/07791849728418b50d59d705e3d959e93383e408886fd319ae3a513a1b7159ba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-3146" :question/id #uuid "5da2e5f1-1de9-5fc2-b99b-9668203d66cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-3146" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/c61d9140e5b8e57c8f75b532c6e26e22faa073e6631984a8f6a7b6f4280882e3.png)\n\nFind the area of the finite region enclosed between $y={x}^{2}+4x$ and $y=x,$ shown above.\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x & =x \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ x(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-3$ to $x=0.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/6b2232d5a2853b22c731c317b365822e371f3d419bfcb3e528a07f211a372553.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}+4x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}[\\,(x)-({x}^{2}+4x)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}]-[-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-3)^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-3)^{2}] \\\\  & =(0)-(9-\\frac{27}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-3146/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa1bebf-4af0-5a5c-962c-8a88f8392776"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-3146"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-121580/selection/5c0598f19f8324c341654ad8fd4df667dbb52f406431a30b6711906dc66d3ee4" :answer/id #uuid "c05dfeb1-ce69-557d-b5e6-784020e18874" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121580/selection/3364379d57456630742eec9aa3a821bc7801b14643c808746601c8281d43aa9e" :answer/id #uuid "599e765b-9113-5edf-a098-b794cd8acf05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{250}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121580/selection/198e23089eaac2c24aa0521b5e4270d877579d1783a35cc741dd46af60d91e60" :answer/id #uuid "f11d683b-c1a7-5ea2-a1b2-44e326c96f65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121580/selection/d8180fb40af5867fc6ed0537e9ad01a16006bf53f8c78b6e3cc5f547b07ffefb" :answer/id #uuid "32667257-77f9-5c35-9ad5-917ddd15e307" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121580/selection/e6e23435513addf3db1915cec122ee224944a546f8b89650ff565ac76ded62dd" :answer/id #uuid "dad44faf-cc92-54f9-a63d-8f7f63e83ce3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{157}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-121580/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "887f178a-f341-58f2-a1a5-1775c0807647" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-121580/selection/d8180fb40af5867fc6ed0537e9ad01a16006bf53f8c78b6e3cc5f547b07ffefb" :answer-field/choices ["q-121580/selection/5c0598f19f8324c341654ad8fd4df667dbb52f406431a30b6711906dc66d3ee4" "q-121580/selection/3364379d57456630742eec9aa3a821bc7801b14643c808746601c8281d43aa9e" "q-121580/selection/198e23089eaac2c24aa0521b5e4270d877579d1783a35cc741dd46af60d91e60" "q-121580/selection/d8180fb40af5867fc6ed0537e9ad01a16006bf53f8c78b6e3cc5f547b07ffefb" "q-121580/selection/e6e23435513addf3db1915cec122ee224944a546f8b89650ff565ac76ded62dd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-121580" :question/id #uuid "49edfe25-0508-5110-9496-2725f5f95615" :question/math-academy-id "q-121580" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}-9$ and $y=7.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/85961197a76cc38713537f80a9c97bb158247c71a8be4633c98245ea158634b4.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-4$ to $x=4.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/7543876e9138be135630313317e162c9b256713f2565b0cd3b5a24bb9ba9a3b3.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-9,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=7.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(7)-({x}^{2}-9)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(16x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =[16\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}]-[16\\cdot (-4)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-4)^{3}] \\\\  & =(64-\\frac{64}{3})-(-64+\\frac{64}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-121580/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa1bebf-4af0-5a5c-962c-8a88f8392776"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-121580"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-11488/selection/f48b848c0ba9eb972a6ecdade8ce516585f3a0e3b5dbeab089775adb9307dc15" :answer/id #uuid "197a676d-a3fa-5e3f-ab32-4e6eabf55b56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-11488/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "0f0603b0-b608-5b2d-a2e2-9263798a3e11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-11488/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer/id #uuid "707b79b8-625e-5731-ac5e-d62db9e56406" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-11488/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "d890a635-43b5-5954-bc9c-0b6ecb2eaa4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-11488/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" :answer/id #uuid "9feb6521-4acd-5629-b935-c878f5ed613c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-11488/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e5afbd31-dfe4-5306-a41f-19a0229cf16a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-11488/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" :answer-field/choices ["q-11488/selection/f48b848c0ba9eb972a6ecdade8ce516585f3a0e3b5dbeab089775adb9307dc15" "q-11488/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-11488/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" "q-11488/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-11488/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-11488" :question/id #uuid "d7acbd35-2c0b-57d1-af2a-99344b438f27" :question/math-academy-id "q-11488" :question/problem "Find the area of the finite region enclosed between $y=4{x}^{2}-1$ and $y=3{x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/4e3758ddf652c3dd7df10d381b42ca1dde50d48250bed672b740329191202597.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/54ad3b4c8bc3aef707af764cd8d8badf9390737dc75491aa66ea554f531a7576.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=4{x}^{2}-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=3{x}^{2}.$\n\nWe can now set up the integration with our $x$ values (Top-Bottom):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(3{x}^{2})-(4{x}^{2}-1)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the integrand is an even function. Therefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{(x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2((1-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3})-0) \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-11488/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa1bebf-4af0-5a5c-962c-8a88f8392776"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-11488"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-351944/field-1/5c0598f19f8324c341654ad8fd4df667dbb52f406431a30b6711906dc66d3ee4" :answer/id #uuid "bfed83e3-a255-594e-a670-b12bc21afbf5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351944/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4d39581b-30e9-596a-af6d-072df58968c5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351944/field-1/5c0598f19f8324c341654ad8fd4df667dbb52f406431a30b6711906dc66d3ee4" :answer-field/choices ["q-351944/field-1/5c0598f19f8324c341654ad8fd4df667dbb52f406431a30b6711906dc66d3ee4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351944" :question/id #uuid "b345d94e-b06b-524d-aa98-24568478ff38" :question/math-academy-id "q-351944" :question/problem "Find the area $A$ of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}+6$ and $y=10.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/5ba7f7b4022c35c4afe7d285c57f87d6fcebfbcd8373ddc530f849e29fbc22de.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6 & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/3ff79baa7d3d934dd9815b398cbd9f56990011370408ddede51e3c4110b4978c.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}+6,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=10.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(10)-({x}^{2}+6)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(4x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =[4\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{3}]-[4\\cdot (-2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-2)^{3}] \\\\  & =(8-\\frac{8}{3})-(-8+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-351944/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa1bebf-4af0-5a5c-962c-8a88f8392776"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-351944"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-552"] :question/problem "Write down an integral that gives the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=\\sqrt x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/b98474e6de2c94876a75758d56a7fb356de22bccd029bfeed0207affebab09ce.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=0$ and $x=1.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/1b1c2b1826fa988ba23f7270b4c8bbdc91bf03066e8ca3638def329466d5b93e.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\sqrt x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\sqrt{x})-({x}^{2})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{x}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-554"] :question/problem "What is the integral that gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=x^2+x+5$ and $y=x+9?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/5f6980e3a7e4c7d1a5cc5d9b93a0110764b34ea6b7a4bec7c828f04cef842f7f.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x+5 & =x+9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/3c52f1405079380459e60ad35e05218d1f0468ee177f1e383498c672d48eaa8d.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2+x+5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x+9.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(x+9)-({x}^{2}+x+5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-553"] :question/problem "Find the area of the finite region enclosed between $f(x) =x^2-2x+2$ and $g(x) = 2,$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/01f139c53b6328341b463f5ad941d37fccb241fff109c0584207017341e8e5f7.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+2 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/39798ef4ff6e898527864628dc2750f88b115c03b2d6b8fb729f30b21d2d2bf5.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2-2x+2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(2)-({x}^{2}-2x+2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =({2}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{3})-(0-0) \\\\  & =(4-\\frac{8}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
